import Mathlib.FieldTheory.Separable
import Mathlib.RingTheory.AlgebraicIndependent.TranscendenceBasis
import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.FiniteDimensional.Basic

/-!
# Finite separable subextensions from a transcendence basis

Source: literature/stacks-project/algebra.tex, proof of Lemma Tag 030U,
lines 10187--10191 (blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-separating-transcendence-basis-finite-subextension).
-/

universe u v w

/--
For finitely many elements of a separable algebraic extension, a finite subset of a
transcendence basis suffices to give a finite separable subextension.

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, proof of Lemma Tag 030U
(`lemma-separable-extension-preserves-reducedness`), second paragraph, lines 10187--10191.
The source asserts this claim without proof; the DAG supplies the following argument.

1. Put `M = k(x_i; i ∈ I)`. Separability over `M` gives a monic minimal polynomial
   `P_j` for each `a_j` and polynomials `U_j`, `V_j` with
   `U_j * P_j + V_j * P_j' = 1`. The coefficients of these finitely many polynomials
   form a finite set `C`.
2. By `lem-weil-pbc-adjoin-element-finite-subset`, each coefficient lies in the field
   generated by finitely many `x_i`. Choose their indices and take the finite union `T`.
   Then `L = k(x_i; i ∈ T)` contains `C` and is contained in `M`. If `n = 0`, take
   `T = ∅` and `L = k`.
3. The polynomials and their Bézout identities descend to `L[T]`, since its inclusion
   into `M[T]` is injective and commutes with differentiation. Each nonzero `P_j`
   vanishes at `a_j`. Its minimal polynomial over `L` divides `P_j`; rewriting the
   Bézout identity through this divisibility proves that minimal polynomial separable.
   The minimal polynomial is unchanged when `a_j` is viewed in `K' = L(a_1, …, a_n)`,
   because the inclusions into `K` are injective.
4. By `lem-weil-pbc-algebraic-generators-finite-extension`, adjoining the finitely many
   algebraic `a_j` makes `K'/L` finite-dimensional. Every element of `K'` is algebraic:
   linear dependence of its first `d + 1` powers, where `d` is the dimension, supplies
   a nonzero polynomial vanishing at it.
5. By `lem-weil-pbc-separable-first-purely-inseparable`, the elements of `K'` separable
   over `L` form its relative separable closure, a subfield containing `L` and every
   `a_j`. The minimality of the generated field makes this subfield all of `K'`.
   Thus `K'/L` is separable algebraic as well as finite-dimensional.
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-separating-transcendence-basis-finite-subextension)
-/
theorem separating_transcendence_basis_finite_subextension
    (k : Type u) (K : Type v) [Field k] [Field K] [Algebra k K]
    (I : Type w) (x : I → K)
    (hx : IsTranscendenceBasis k x)
    (hsep : Algebra.IsSeparable (IntermediateField.adjoin k (Set.range x)) K)
    (n : ℕ) (a : Fin n → K) :
    ∃ T : Finset I,
      FiniteDimensional
        (IntermediateField.adjoin k (x '' (↑T : Set I)))
        (IntermediateField.adjoin
          (IntermediateField.adjoin k (x '' (↑T : Set I))) (Set.range a)) ∧
      Algebra.IsSeparable
        (IntermediateField.adjoin k (x '' (↑T : Set I)))
        (IntermediateField.adjoin
          (IntermediateField.adjoin k (x '' (↑T : Set I))) (Set.range a)) := by
  sorry
